1
00:00:05,710 --> 00:00:09,710
Understanding compressed gas cylinder markings.

2
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:17,710
Knowing how to find the testing date on your compressed gas cylinders and understanding their other markings are essential if you use them for research.

3
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:31,710
Compressed gas cylinders are stamped to denote certain specifications, such as how the cylinder was made, the unique serial number, the date the cylinder was made, the owner of the cylinder, when the cylinder was last tested, and more.

4
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:38,790
The neck ring displays the name of the current owner or manufacturer of the cylinder.

5
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:43,790
As you can see on this cylinder, it was manufactured by MG Technical Products.

6
00:00:43,790 --> 00:00:52,380
The data labels should be visible on all compressed gas cylinders and indicate the cylinder's contents.

7
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:55,380
It also tells you what hazards are associated with the cylinder.

8
00:00:55,380 --> 00:01:00,380
This is a cylinder with nitrogen and it is a non-flammable.

9
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:04,380
Never rely on the color of the cylinder to identify the contents.

10
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:15,380
Always read the cylinder label and safety data sheet to identify cylinder contents, hazards, properties, and other safety-related information of the gas cylinder.

11
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:21,380
Different compressed gas cylinder manufacturers and distributors have different color schemes for their products.

12
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:27,380
If the data label is missing, that cylinder should not be used. Contact the distributor immediately.

13
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:40,380
Most Iowa State University researchers on campus do not own their compressed gas cylinders, as this would be very expensive and require the researchers to conduct or contract out their own hydrostatic testing.

14
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:51,380
Researchers can rent cylinders from ISU chemistry stores for about $10 per month as of 2024, in addition to the cost of the cylinder contents.

15
00:01:51,380 --> 00:02:00,750
Gas cylinders must be tested periodically to ensure the safety of those who transport, fill, and use them.

16
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:04,750
Markings on the cylinder indicate the frequency of required testing.

17
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:10,750
For example, a five-pointed star indicates that the next test is required in 10 years.

18
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:19,750
Lab personnel must return the cylinder to the distributor, such as chemistry stores, or the manufacturer for testing according to the date markings on the cylinder.

19
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:26,750
Suppose you see only one date marked on the cylinder. This is the date it was manufactured and first tested.

20
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:32,750
This cylinder was manufactured and tested for the first time on March of 1955.

21
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:40,750
Typically, a cylinder should be tested every five years, but this can vary depending on the composition of the cylinder and the contents stored.

22
00:02:41,750 --> 00:02:46,750
If you see more than one date stamped, these are the retest dates of the cylinder.

23
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:54,750
The format for retest marking is month, facility, year, plus rating, star stamp.

24
00:02:54,750 --> 00:03:00,750
The plus symbol or the plus rating suggests the cylinder qualifies for 10% overfill.

25
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:03,750
For this cylinder, seven retests were done.

26
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:21,750
May of 1962, May of 1969, August of 1992, June of 1998, October of 2008, May of 2014, and the last test date was November of 2021.

27
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:29,750
Since there is no star symbol associated with the last test date, this cylinder will be due for retesting in 2026.

28
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:36,340
You will also find other stamps on your compressed gas cylinder.

29
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:48,340
The alphanumeric number that starts with D-O-T indicates Department of Transportation, the governing agency that put forward the gas cylinder transportation regulations in the United States.

30
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:55,340
You might also notice ICC, Interstate Commerce Commissions, for older U.S. cylinders.

31
00:03:55,340 --> 00:04:02,340
Additionally, you might come across other markings, such as CTC, for Canadian cylinders.

32
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:08,340
That indicate the regulatory Canadian authority under which the cylinder was manufactured.

33
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:13,340
The next set of numbers specifies the type of material used for construction.

34
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:22,340
For example, 3AA or 3A means steel and 3AL means aluminum.

35
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:25,340
Next, you will find the rated pressure in pounds per square inch.

36
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:29,340
Here, it is 2,260 PSI.

37
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:35,340
That tilts you the operating pressure of this compressed gas cylinder is 2,260 pounds per square inch.

38
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:39,340
Then you will see the serial number of the cylinder.

39
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:47,340
You might also see a few other stickers of barcodes that indicate batch numbers and such that distributors have on the cylinders.

40
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:52,340
For more information about gas cylinders, check out the Gas Cylinder Safety Guidelines Manual linked below.

41
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,340
If you have questions, please contact ISU-EHNS.
